body:
Resolves #2014. Supersedes and closes #2037, taking over from where @Beigelman left off.

## What

Adds a `yearStartsOn` option (0-indexed month, default `0`) to `startOfYear`, `endOfYear`, `startOfQuarter`, `endOfQuarter`, `isSameQuarter` and `isSameYear`, so fiscal and academic years that don't begin in January work out of the box:

```ts
// US federal fiscal year (October–September):
startOfYear(new Date(2026, 1 /* Feb */, 15), { yearStartsOn: 9 });
//=> Thu Oct 01 2025 00:00:00
```

## Why a new PR

The original #2037 (opened in 2020) carried the approved approach but pre-dates the TypeScript monorepo, so the diff no longer applies. This PR is a fresh implementation on the current codebase that follows @kossnocorp's review of #2037 point by point:

- options are documented per function (each interface carries the `yearStartsOn` JSDoc, matching how `ContextOptions` composes in the current codebase — there is no shared interface in `types.ts` anymore, so the `YearStartOptions` rename concern no longer applies);
- defaults use `options?.yearStartsOn ?? 0`;
- the quarter-start computation is explained inline right where it happens (the old `diffToQuarterStartArray` lookup is replaced by the arithmetic above it);
- the `isSameYear` "weird condition" from the review is gone — with `yearStartsOn`, dates before the fiscal start month resolve to the previous calendar year, and the comparison is on that effective year.

It also fixes the year-boundary bug reported by @zhirzh in #2037: dates falling before the fiscal start month used to resolve to the wrong calendar year. Both `startOfYear` and `endOfYear` (and the quarter variants) now decrement/increment correctly across the wrap.

## Testing

21 new tests across the six functions' suites covering: the fiscal start itself, the year-boundary wrap (a date before the fiscal start month, e.g. February with `yearStartsOn: 3`), quarters that wrap around December (`yearStartsOn: 9`), `isSameQuarter`/`isSameYear` grouping across the calendar-year boundary, and the unchanged default behavior. Full core suite result is identical to `main` (same 78 pre-existing Temporal-environment failures, none new) plus the 21 new passing tests. `oxlint --type-aware` clean.

Co-authored-by: @Beigelman (the original implementation this takes over).

Japanese narrow month names fail to parse for January and October–December:

```js
import { parse } from "date-fns";
import { ja } from "date-fns/locale";

parse("10", "MMMMM", new Date(1986, 3, 4, 10, 32, 0), { locale: ja });
// Before: Invalid Date
// After: October 1, 1986
```

The narrow matcher consumes the first digit of a two-digit month, and January's parse pattern rejects the bare `1` returned by the matcher. Match two-digit months first and allow January's pattern to end after `1`. Keep the nondigit check from #1807 so October–December cannot be interpreted as January.

Addresses the Japanese narrow-month part of #3136. Other locale and token round-trip failures remain outside this change.

Tested:

- `parse` → `Japanese months`: all twelve months with `MMMMM` and `LLLLL`, narrow months followed by a delimiter and date, and compatibility with `MMM`, `MMMM`, `LLL`, and `LLLL`. The parse suite passed 588 tests; 16 added cases failed before the fix.
- `test/node`: 3,214 passed and 1 skipped in each matrix run; timezone tests passed.
- Types, lint, formatting, and whitespace checks passed.
- The regenerated Japanese snapshot matches its generator. The full snapshot check still fails on existing Chinese snapshot mismatches (`zh-CN`, `zh-HK`, `zh-TW`); those unrelated changes are excluded.

AI assistance: this change was developed with an AI coding tool; I reviewed and tested it.

Added union of `Date | number | string` as a value argument  to TZDate constructor overloads to make it closer to native Date constructor and improve developer ergonomics.

Right now passing an union to the constructor emits a type error even though all union members are valid arguments:
```ts
const foo = (date: Date | string) => new TZDate(value);
// No overload matches this call.
//  Overload 1 of 10, '(dateStr: string, timeZone?: string | undefined): TZDate', gave the following error.
//    Argument of type 'string | Date' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string'.
//      Type 'Date' is not assignable to type 'string'.
//  Overload 2 of 10, '(date: Date, timeZone?: string | undefined): TZDate', gave the following error.
//    Argument of type 'string | Date' is not assignable to parameter of type 'Date'.
//      Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'Date'.
//  Overload 3 of 10, '(timestamp: number, timeZone?: string | undefined): TZDate', gave the following error.
//    Argument of type 'string | Date' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number'.
//      Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'.ts(2769)
```

# What

Follow-up to #4308. While reviewing `parseISO`'s ISO 8601 handling against the spec (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601), I found that week-date validation doesn't account for the fact that most ISO week-years have only 52 weeks:

```diff
-function validateWeekDate(_year: number, week: number, day: number): boolean {
-  return week >= 1 && week <= 53 && day >= 0 && day <= 6;
-}
```

An ISO week-year has 53 weeks only when 1 January falls on a Thursday, or on a Wednesday in a leap year — every other year has 52. `validateWeekDate` accepted any week in `[1, 53]` regardless of the year, so:

```js
parseISO("2021-W53-1")
// 2021 is a 52-week ISO year (1 January 2021 is a Friday) — there is no week 53
// => Sun Jan 02 2022 ...   (silently rolls into the next ISO year instead of Invalid Date)
```

This is the same failure class as #4308: the function's own docstring/contract says an invalid component value returns Invalid Date, but this one silently produces a plausible-looking wrong date instead of signaling invalidity.

# Fix

`validateWeekDate` now checks the week against the actual week count for that ISO week-year via a new `weeksInISOWeekYear` helper (1 January is Thursday, or Wednesday in a leap year, → 53 weeks; otherwise 52):

```diff
-function validateWeekDate(_year: number, week: number, day: number): boolean {
-  return week >= 1 && week <= 53 && day >= 0 && day <= 6;
+function validateWeekDate(year: number, week: number, day: number): boolean {
+  return (
+    week >= 1 && week <= weeksInISOWeekYear(year) && day >= 0 && day <= 6
+  );
 }
+
+function weeksInISOWeekYear(isoWeekYear: number): number {
+  const januaryFirst = new Date(0);
+  januaryFirst.setUTCFullYear(isoWeekYear, 0, 1);
+  const januaryFirstDay = januaryFirst.getUTCDay();
+
+  if (
+    januaryFirstDay === 4 ||
+    (januaryFirstDay === 3 && isLeapYearIndex(isoWeekYear))
+  ) {
+    return 53;
+  }
+
+  return 52;
+}
```

# Testing

Added two tests to `parseISO/test.ts`'s existing week-validation `describe` block:
- `parseISO("2021-W53-1")` → Invalid Date (2021 has only 52 ISO weeks)
- `parseISO("2020-W53-1")` → correctly parses (2020 genuinely has 53 ISO weeks), asserting it resolves to `Dec 28 2020`

60/60 in the file passing (58 unaffected). Full `pkgs/core` suite: 3214 passing, 1 skipped, no regressions.

`oxfmt` formatting applied and clean.

## Bug

`tzOffset` returns a positive offset for time zones whose UTC offset is between -00:01 and -00:59, flipping the sign. This corrupts `TZDate` for those zones:

```js
import { tzOffset, TZDate } from "@date-fns/tz";

// Liberia used UTC-00:44:30 until January 1972
tzOffset("Africa/Monrovia", new Date("1970-01-15T00:00:00Z"));
//=> 44.5 (expected -44.5)

tzOffset("-00:30", new Date("2020-01-15T00:00:00Z"));
//=> 30 (expected -30)

new TZDate(1970, 0, 1, 12, 0, "Africa/Monrovia").toISOString();
// treats the zone as UTC+00:44:30, so the result is off by 89 minutes
```

Any LMT-era date in a zone slightly west of Greenwich hits the same path, e.g. `Europe/Dublin` before 1880 is -00:25:21 but comes back as +25.35. Zones at -01:00 or beyond (like the tested 1880 `America/New_York` LMT) are unaffected.

## Root cause

In `calcOffset` (`pkgs/tz/src/tzOffset/index.ts`), the sign is derived from the parsed numbers:

```ts
hours * 60 + minutes > 0
  ? hours * 60 + minutes + seconds
  : hours * 60 - minutes - seconds;
```

For `"-00:44:30"`, `+"-00"` evaluates to `-0`, so `hours * 60 + minutes` is `44` — positive — and the minutes/seconds get added instead of subtracted.

## Fix

Read the sign from the hours string (`"-00"`) instead of the number, then apply it to the absolute value. Both the `Intl` path and the manual `±HH:MM`/`±HHMM`/`±HH` fallback go through `calcOffset`, so both are covered.

## Tests

Added to `pkgs/tz/src/tzOffset/tests.ts`:

- `Africa/Monrovia` in 1970 → `-44.5` (Intl path, seconds-bearing offset)
- `"-00:30"` → `-30` and `"-0030"` → `-30` (offset string formats)

All three fail before the fix (returning `+44.5` / `+30`) and pass after; the rest of the `pkgs/tz` suite is unaffected.
